/*! \page page_csv AnCH CSV library documentation
 *
 * \tableofcontents
 *
 * \section csv_intro_seq Introduction
 *
 * AnCH CSV library reads and writes CSV records (RFC 4180) as streams, without loading the whole input, and maps
 * records to objects with their description shared by formats ( \c anch::Mapping , anch-utils), one by one (range)
 * or in vectors.
 *
 * \section csv_install_sec Installation
 *
 * CMake target \c anch::csv (option \c ANCH_BUILD_CSV , ON):
 * \code{.cmake}
find_package(Anch REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(my-app PRIVATE anch::csv)
\endcode
 * See \ref install_sec "installation".
 *
 * \section csv_format_sec Format
 *
 * - RFC 4180 by default: \c , separator, fields quoted with \c " when they contain the separator, a quote, CR or
 *   LF, quotes doubled in quoted fields, records written with \c CRLF .
 * - Reading accepts \c CRLF and \c LF line endings ( \c CR alone is refused), a last record without line ending,
 *   and skips an UTF-8 BOM. An empty field and an empty quoted field ( \c "" ) are distinguished
 *   ( \ref anch::csv::Reader::quoted "quoted").
 * - Bytes are kept as is (UTF-8 is expected), without conversion.
 * - Limits: record size ( \c Options::maxRecordSize , 1 MiB) and number of columns ( \c Options::maxColumns , 1024) ;
 *   every record has the number of columns of the header or of the first record ( \c Options::sameColumnCount ).
 *
 * \section csv_write_sec Writing
 *
 * \ref anch::csv::Writer "Writer" quotes fields when needed. \c writeRow writes strings as is, booleans as \c true /
 * \c false and numbers with \c std::to_chars (independent of the locale):
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-csv/examples/csv.cpp writer
 *
 * \section csv_read_sec Reading
 *
 * \ref anch::csv::Reader "Reader" reads one record at a time: fields are \c std::string_view valid until the next
 * record (the record buffer is reused). Errors raise \ref anch::csv::CsvError "CsvError" with a code, the line and
 * the column:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-csv/examples/csv.cpp reader
 *
 * \section csv_objects_sec Objects
 *
 * Objects are described once for every format ( \c anch::Mapping ): fields are columns. CSV objects are flat: fields
 * are strings, booleans, numbers, \c anch::UUID , dates (RFC 3339) or \c std::optional of them ; nested objects and
 * collections are refused at compile time.
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-csv/examples/csv.cpp mapping
 *
 * \ref anch::csv::ObjectWriter "ObjectWriter" writes the header, then one record per object. An absent value
 * ( \c std::nullopt ) is an empty field, an empty string an empty quoted field ( \c "" ):
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-csv/examples/csv.cpp objects writer
 *
 * \ref anch::csv::ObjectReader "ObjectReader" associates columns with fields by name (or by position without
 * header). Columns which are not fields are ignored ( \c Options::ignoreUnknownColumns ), required fields
 * ( \c anch::val::required ) columns have to be present. Conversion errors and anch-val constraints violations
 * ( \c Options::readCheckConstraints ) raise \ref anch::csv::CsvError "CsvError" with the line and the column:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-csv/examples/csv.cpp objects reader
 *
 * \section csv_records_sec Records ranges and vectors
 *
 * \ref anch::csv::records "records" reads objects one by one, without loading the whole input (input range, read
 * once: errors are raised while iterating) ; \ref anch::csv::deserialize "deserialize" and
 * \ref anch::csv::serialize "serialize" read and write vectors (and any range) for small volumes:
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-csv/examples/csv.cpp records
 *
 * \section csv_options_sec Options
 *
 * \ref anch::csv::Options "Options" set separator, quote, line ending, header, BOM, limits and objects mapping
 * (unknown columns, constraints checks, booleans texts):
 * \snippet{trimleft} anch-csv/examples/csv.cpp options
 */
